// R11 gap 1 (docs/OWNERSHIP.md, "Still broken" table, row 1): a Cell body
// never releases its own `arc` parameter, because no parameter is dropped
// (`Local.droppable` excludes every parameter, arc included), so every
// reference handed into one leaks. `examples/arc_host.c`'s `cell_observe` is
// the ABI-correct contrast: it honours `runtime/cell_rt.h` section 7 by
// dropping the `arc` parameter it is handed, which is exactly what a
// Cell-bodied `consume` below never does.
//
// `make()` returns an already-retained `arc String` (R11 release rule 3, the
// same shape as `examples/codegen tests` "a call that returns arc is bound
// without a second retain"); passed to `consume`'s `arc` parameter, the
// value is not itself a PLACE, so R11 retain rule 2's clone does not fire
// either (nothing to clone: the callee simply receives the one reference
// `make()` already produced) and `consume` then does nothing with it. Each
// call leaks exactly the one box `make()` allocated.
//
// This fixture ISOLATES that gap alone: `make()`'s own `let arc a` is
// declared directly in `make`'s body, not nested under a loop, so the
// block-scoped-local gap (examples/leaks/block_scoped_local.cell) cannot
// also fire here, and nothing here is a struct field or a `shared` unbox.
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it (arc is
// scalar-first-refused there, same as examples/arc.cell); lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level examples/*.cell
// corpus, agreement, and mlir-lowering loops (see examples/leaks/README.md).

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn make() -> arc String {
    let arc a = "x"
    return a
}

pub fn consume(arc s: String) {
}

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        consume(arc make())
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
